WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.550
- [Narrator] Amazon, Ford,

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:06.210
Pepsi, and more from Amazon.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.890
These are just a few of the companies

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.840
that have said they were
starting big layoffs

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.190
in the last year.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.780
Some job cuts are expected

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.820
when the economy hits a rough patch,

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.590
but this is different,

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.470
not because of the number
of people being laid off,

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.880
but because of who.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.070
- We're seeing a really unusual situation

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.640
where white-collar
professionals are being laid off

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.550
in greater numbers relative
to the rest of the workforce.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.780
- [Narrator] Here's why white-collar jobs

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.160
are getting cut first,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.983
and why that's a departure
from slowdowns in the past.

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:37.350
(gentle instrumental music)

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.896
Ever since this wave of
job cuts started last year,

00:00:40.896 --> 00:00:43.140
experts have said that this
downturn could spell trouble

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.700
for professional workers.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.520
- This recession is gonna be really bad,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.020
not only for white-collar workers,

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.920
but white Patagonia vested workers.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.290
- [Narrator] But to understand
why those cuts are happening,

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.210
we have to go back even further

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.980
to the beginning of the pandemic.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.140
As the economy went into crisis mode,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.900
layoffs hit a lot of industries,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.300
but ones that are not
predominantly white-collar

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.310
were some of the most affected.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.690
Entertainment, and leisure,

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.300
and retail which hire a lot of low wage

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.000
and frontline staff made
up most of the job cuts

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.520
in the first seven months of 2020.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.430
- In-person workplaces,
everybody got sent home,

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.070
and so you had a lot
of white-collar workers

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.190
who could do the kinds of jobs
that companies still needed

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.090
in great quantity.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.770
And companies like Zoom boomed,

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.900
companies like Google and Microsoft,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.480
suddenly their products
were in much greater demand.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.580
- [Narrator] As the
economy started to recover,

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.550
some industry saw the average
number of employees grow

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.280
from pre-pandemic levels, while others,

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.440
especially those with frontline workers,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.070
grew only a little bit or
were left at a deficit.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.530
But as employers are now making job cuts

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.720
amid rising interest rates,

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.090
they're facing two major problems.

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.430
For some, it's an excess
of white-collar workers

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.770
who may have been hired
for special projects

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.260
or company expansions during the pandemic.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.080
- Some of those projects have
been closed, or pulled back,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.230
or narrowed, and so
companies that hired a lot

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.330
during the pandemic are just finding

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.370
they don't need as many people.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.300
- [Narrator] But elsewhere,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.550
there's an unmet demand for workers,

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.140
namely in blue-collar,

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.240
low wage, and frontline jobs.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.825
- It feels like we have

00:02:19.825 --> 00:02:21.930
a structural labor shortage
out there where there are,

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.550
you know, 4 million fewer people,

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.050
a little more than 4 million,

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.120
who are in the workforce available to work

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.740
than there's demand for workforce.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.590
- [Narrator] The Fed says the
shortage has multiple causes.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.620
Some of it is due to more
people going into retirement,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.080
but other factors like slowing immigration

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.120
are also playing a role.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.760
Some economists also say that mindsets

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.483
about work have shifted amid
COVID-19 for these workers.

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.260
If we look back at that change
in employees on payroll,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.640
some of the industries that have had

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.710
a harder time recovering are ones

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.840
that a lot of workers didn't return to.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.150
- And companies don't
want to risk being unable

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.810
to fill important jobs as
the economy speeds up again.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.870
So they're being careful
about keeping those workers,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.850
some people call it labor hoarding,

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.307
they're actually holding
onto these people,

00:03:08.307 --> 00:03:09.650
trying to keep them from leaving,

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.310
even if they don't need
them all right now.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.290
- [Narrator] As hiring
has started to slow,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.390
ads for jobs in white-collar industries

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:16.710
have fallen the most.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.780
And as for layoffs.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.090
- White-collar workers are
there, they're paid more,

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.480
so job cuts can save you
more money with fewer people,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.910
and you don't risk giving up

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:30.210
those hard to find frontline workers

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.120
that you're gonna need
when the economy improves.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.410
- [Narrator] But this breaks from the norm

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.830
of what has happened in many
other economic slowdowns.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.334
Usually.

00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:41.190
- First capital intensive
industries lay people off,

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.500
factories and construction companies,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.260
then you see companies with
large low wage workforces,

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.390
retailers, hospitality companies,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.100
restaurants and hotels.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.340
And only later, as the
economy really starts to slow

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.610
and as a recession looms or kicks in

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.020
are professional workers

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.420
and white-collar workers usually laid off.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.060
- [Narrator] Just look at the
recession in the early 1980s

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.840
where blue-collar workers
made up 75% of layoffs.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.880
- It's few and far between,
jobs are hard to come.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.050
But how about you, you hiring?

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.840
- [Narrator] And even
more recently, in 2007,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.150
just before the start
of the great recession,

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.150
blue-collar industries like
construction and manufacturing,

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.580
were some of the first
to start losing jobs.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:24.330
By the end of 2008 when mass
layoffs were gaining steam,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.760
industries that are not
predominantly white-collar

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.580
were some that had the
most job cut announcements,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.850
that's excluding finance,
which was in a crisis.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.890
- So the classic model we've seen over

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.590
the past 38 to 40 years
is that when companies

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:40.260
see a slowdown coming, they shed workers.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.250
They figure, you know,

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.160
we can always hire them back
once business picks up again.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.610
But that didn't happen after the pandemic.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.950
- [Narrator] But even
if white-collar workers

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.300
are mostly affected by this downturn,

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.673
they don't typically
stay unemployed for long.

00:04:54.934 --> 00:04:57.372
The majority of tech
workers who were laid off

00:04:57.372 --> 00:04:58.672
this year landed a new job

00:04:59.595 --> 00:05:00.630
within three months of
starting their search.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.300
- Some smaller companies are
finding it easier to hire now,

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.280
because you know, there's
more people on the market.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.740
So just because there's a lot of layoffs

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.270
doesn't mean no one's hiring.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:10.620
- [Narrator] Despite the layoffs,

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.710
the job market remains strong
by historical standards.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.260
The Fed is still looking
for it to cool further.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.230
- The economists I've spoken
with expect more layoffs.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.600
They don't expect it to be
like in the financial crisis

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.880
when you saw pretty widespread layoffs,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.220
but they do expect layoffs
to spread from, you know,

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.050
tech and the white-collar

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:31.980
to other areas where the
economy may slow down next.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:33.720
- [Narrator] Some
economists say that layoffs

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:37.230
could become more precisely
targeted at underperformers,

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:38.610
as companies try to hold on to

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.010
their most valuable employees.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.220
(gentle instrumental music)

